This instance began with a salient 7-shaped motif recolored to 9 in the first demo, then showed a second different local recoloring (1 to 5) and a third (5 to 9) while many unrelated shapes remained untouched. The initial test guess simply recolored selected 7-motif occurrences and was wrong. These pairs do not justify extrapolating one template-copy or ray rule from their surface similarities.

For heterogeneous examples, record each complete diff and negative evidence, then seek a shared structural relation that accounts for all pairs. Separate trigger/scope, spatial selection, eligible input value, output color, and preservation behavior. Test against every demo before spending another demo request or submitting. If a common rule is not yet evident, do not continue reflexive demo chains: name alternative hypotheses and the specific evidence another example could distinguish.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u089/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/mixed-demo-integration-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/local-demo-global-rule.md", "content": "# Separating a demo's local effect from its global rule

A minimal example may reveal what happens to particular cells without proving how those cells are selected in larger grids. In this session, the 2×10 pair established that a trailing run of 9s becomes 8s and that part of a run remains unchanged, but it did not validate a whole-grid operation for a separate 22×25 test. The first submission simply erased the test's 1-cells; rejection falsified that extrapolation. The next submission erased all test marks except an isolated vertical pair, without a demonstration supporting that selection, and was also unsupported.

When demo and test scales differ sharply, separate and validate: (1) local value mapping, (2) trigger/selection, (3) propagation or geometry, and (4) scope. Keep unchanged demo cells as constraints. If multiple global generators still fit, request a demo designed to distinguish them rather than submitting a conjecture. Never infer an exception mask solely from a visually salient test pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u089/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/local-demo-global-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md", "content": "# Cellwise mask validation across heterogeneous demos

When an ARC task has a distinctive colored seed in noisy grids, do not equate resemblance with the rule. Track the seed shape, eligible target values, and spatial generator as independent hypotheses. For every demo, enumerate the full input/output difference mask and preserve negative evidence: unchanged lookalikes, untouched target-valued cells, and the original seed.

Before another demo or a submission, replay the proposed generator against every pair, especially when seed shape, palette, or dimensions vary. A literal translated-template matcher that looks plausible on one example but fails the varied demos is falsified; do not patch it by shifting a few cells. Request additional examples only for explicit remaining alternatives and a specific discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u089/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u089/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}
